Jukka Lahtinen <jukkal@despammed.com> writes:

> Pasi Kallinen <pkalli@cs.joensuu.fi> writes:
> > Jukka Lahtinen <jukkal@despammed.com> wrote:
> 
> > > if [ "$TERM" == xterm  -a  -f $HOME/.nethackrc.x11 ] ; then
> > >      NETHACKOPTIONS=$HOME/.nethackrc.x11
> > > fi
> 
> > Wouldn't it be sensible to check that $DISPLAY is set, too?
> 
> I guess it would be a good idea. 
> But why isn't the "$TERM" == xterm test enough? Does something set $TERM
> to xterm even outside of X11?

$TERM might not be set to "xterm" for various reasons (maybe it's
"xterm-color" or "xterm-r6" or somesuch).

$DISPLAY is a reliable indicator of whether you are running in X, and
you can even use the NETHACKOPTIONS environment variable to pass in
which windowtype you want so you don't have to keep a separate
.nethackrc.  Here's what I do to my nethack.sh:

--- nethack.sh  Fri Aug 29 17:08:04 2003
+++ nethack     Sat Sep 20 18:27:00 2003
@@ -56,6 +56,27 @@
        fi
 fi
 
+case "x$DISPLAY" in
+x)
+       WIN=tty
+       ;;
+*)
+       WIN=x11
+       ;;
+esac
+
+case "x$NETHACKOPTIONS" in
+x)
+       NETHACKOPTIONS="windowtype:$WIN"
+       export NETHACKOPTIONS
+       ;;
+*)
+       if echo "$NETHACKOPTIONS" | grep -v "windowtype:" >/dev/null 2>&1
+       then
+               NETHACKOPTIONS="$NETHACKOPTIONS,windowtype:$WIN"
+       fi
+       ;;
+esac
 
 cd $HACKDIR
 case $1 in

